%[text] d) (5 marks) You want to design state-variable feedback controller that has 18% overshoot and settling time of 2 seconds for plant.
%[text] What are the desired locations of the 2 dominant closed loop poles of the system?
% Your code goes here
% Desired specs
pos = 0.18;      % 18% overshoot
T_s = 2;         % 2 s settling time (2% criterion)

% Solve for damping ratio zeta from PO formula
zeta = -log(pos) / sqrt(pi^2 + (log(pos))^2) %[output:389fa533]
% 
% % Natural frequency from Ts ≈ 4/(zeta*wn)
wn = 4/(zeta*T_s);
% 
% % Desired dominant poles
sigma = -zeta*wn;
wd    = wn*sqrt(1 - zeta^2);
p1 = sigma + 1j*wd;
p2 = sigma - 1j*wd;

p1, p2 %[output:31e1a374] %[output:08cc480d]
%[text] Where would you place the other two poles? Why.
%[text] **Place the remaining two poles significantly farther left on the real axis than the dominant pair, e.g.:**
%[text] - **s\_3= -10**
%[text] - **s\_4= -12** \
%[text] **Reason:**
%[text] **Poles far to the left (at least 5 time farther left than the dominant pair) have much faster dynamics (large negative real part), so their modes decay quickly and do not significantly affect the transient response. This keeps the closed‑loop behavior effectively governed by the designed dominant pair at -2 + j\*3.66 and -2 - j\*3.66, preserving the desired 18% overshoot and 2 s settling time. Keeping both poles at the same location is risky because it makes the system much less robust and produces worse time‑domain behaviour than two nearby distinct poles. So, keep one of them at -10 and the other at -12 (keep both on the real axis because it ensures no oscillation, redictable, monotonic step response, simpler tuning and interpretation).** 
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_55259FDD] e) (5 marks) Use whatever matlab command(s) you want to confirm that your statespace realization from part 4(d) is controlllable and then compute a state feedback control matrix $\\mathbf{K}$ to place the poles at the desired locations from 4(c) with the state feedback control $u(t)=-\\mathbf{K}x(t)$.
%[text] 
% Your code goes here
%% Choose remaining poles (fast, real, non-dominant)
p3 = -10; % hardcoded based on (d)'s reasoning
p4 = -12; % hardcoded based on (d)'s reasoning

desired_poles = [p1 p2 p3 p4];

%% Check controllability
Co = ctrb(A,B);
rank_Co = rank(Co) %[output:4b379041]

if rank_Co == size(A,1) %[output:group:41d1eb2c]
    disp('System is controllable') %[output:85dc06a2]
else
    disp('System is NOT controllable')
end %[output:group:41d1eb2c]

%% Compute state feedback gain K
K = place(A, B, desired_poles) %[output:659084ac]
